      "url": "https://www.science.org/content/article/extinct-vampire-camel-once-stalked-north-american-plains",
      "title": "Museum fossils identify 13-million-year-old North American ‘vampire camel’",
      "content_html": "Paleontologists have formally named *Vampyrocamelus welshi*, a long-legged camel that lived on the southern Great Plains about 13 million years ago. Fossils found mainly in Texas and Oklahoma indicate that it had a giraffe-like body and unusually large, backward-curving teeth that inspired its “vampire camel” nickname.\n\nResearchers identified the species from museum fossils, including skulls, jaws and limb bones, by comparing them with those of similar extinct camels. The formal description helps researchers recognize the animal in other fossil collections; the function of its teeth remains uncertain.",
