      "id": "https://www.leparisien.fr/environnement/climat/climat-el-nino-continue-de-sintensifier-et-devrait-atteindre-un-pic-en-decembre-08-10-2026-SMTSZ7FANZA4XA3AJFSBMIAE5M.php",
      "url": "https://www.leparisien.fr/environnement/climat/climat-el-nino-continue-de-sintensifier-et-devrait-atteindre-un-pic-en-decembre-08-10-2026-SMTSZ7FANZA4XA3AJFSBMIAE5M.php",
      "title": "Powerful El Niño nears December peak as droughts, fires threaten crops",
      "content_html": "The World Meteorological Organization says the powerful El Niño underway in the tropical Pacific is expected to peak in December and likely persist through February 2027, disrupting rainfall and raising drought and flood risks worldwide. Sea-surface temperatures in the key Niño 3.4 region rose from 1.6°C above normal in June to 2.5°C above normal in August; forecasters project temperatures about 3.7°C above normal in October-December, exceeding the previous record of 2.6°C above normal.\n\nRecent impacts include drought and fires in Brazil, extensive forest fires in Indonesia, and a possible 21% to 25% decline in Ivory Coast cocoa output. Researchers attributed Indonesia’s July-October rainfall deficit largely to El Niño, while saying human-caused climate change made the atmospheric conditions about three times more likely and more intense. The United Nations Development Programme says acting now to limit the impacts would cost $36 billion, compared with $73 billion if action is delayed.",
      "date_published": "2026-11-17T07:13:07.942000+00:00",
