      "content_html": "<div><p>Source: <a href=\"https://www.science.org/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Science</a></p>Researchers are turning the United Kingdom's upcoming social media ban for children under the age of 16 into a major scientific opportunity. Ten research teams will assess the ban's effects in what amounts to the world's largest study of social media and mental health. Many researchers hope the effort—which will follow more than 300,000 young people, and, in some cases, their parents—will bring much-needed evidence to the hotly debated topic.</div><h6 style=\"clear:both;padding:8px 0 0 0;height:2px;font-size:1px;border:0;margin:0;padding:0;\"></h6><br/><a href=\"https://www.socialpsychology.org/client/redirect.php?action=rssHomepage\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"Brought to you by SocialPsychology Network\" height=\"45\" src=\"https://www.socialpsychology.org/images/rss-footer-large.png\" title=\"Brought to you by Social Psychology Network\" width=\"400\"/></a><br/><br/>",
